# Vertex product design

Owner brief (keep for every future UI change):

Apple-like sites feel advanced because they are **quiet**, not because they use a secret stack. Vertex apps should be **simplistic and futuristic together**.

## Daily chrome

- One job per screen. Do not stack a cutover banner, news ticker, FX rates, Inbox, Focus, Refresh, LIVE, and Legacy in one header.
- Cutover / “awaiting approval” banners and a **Legacy** jump are parallel-run tools — keep them out of the daily header (overflow / docs is fine).
- Industry news marquee is not default chrome.
- LIVE, Refresh, FX, Inbox, Focus stay reachable in **one compact control cluster**.
- Onboarding hints (Ask Vertex / ⌘K) live on Home, not repeated as header copy.

## Look

- Atlas Logistics logo sits beside **ATLAS PRICING** in the sidebar (and login / quotation letterhead).
- Palette (current): dusk navy `#1f2a44` for chrome; calm air-blue `#4d7ca3` for links/hovers; gallery white `#f5f6f8` surfaces. Logo yellow stays in the mark — not as big filled buttons.
- **Glass / depth** (blur, thin border, one soft shadow) is allowed on chrome, cards, and overlays.
- Motion only when it means something. Honor `prefers-reduced-motion`.

## Premium techniques — ask first

A / B / C were **examples**, not a closed menu. Before adding spectacle to desks, show a **sample image referenced to this app** and wait for which treatment to ship.

Never add 3D/scroll spectacle that slows a desk or hides POL/POD/cargo fields.

## Design glossary (plain language → technical name)

Use these words with the owner. They can pick any mix for Vertex or later apps.

### Always-on (already in Vertex)

- **Visual hierarchy** — the eye hits the job first, then the next action, then chrome.
- **Whitespace / negative space** — empty padding so the screen does not feel crowded.
- **Type scale** — a few planned sizes (eyebrow, title, body, caption) instead of random font sizes.
- **Color tokens** — named colors (`--color-atlas-navy`) so the whole app stays consistent.
- **Contrast / WCAG** — dark text on light (or white on navy) so type stays readable.
- **Accent vs brand color** — logo may have yellow; buttons and fills do not have to copy it.
- **Glassmorphism** — frosted panel: translucent fill + `backdrop-filter: blur`.
- **Elevation / depth** — thin border + one soft shadow so a card sits above the page.
- **Sticky nav** — header stays put while the page scrolls.
- **Skeleton loaders** — grey placeholders while quotes/inbox load.
- **Micro-interactions** — small hover/press feedback (button darkens, chip highlights).
- **`prefers-reduced-motion`** — skip animation when the OS asks for less motion.

### Showpiece (sample first)

- **Hero** — a large opening block (Quote hub navy panel is a small hero).
- **Bento grid** — a magazine-like mosaic of unequal tiles (Home metrics could become this).
- **Scrollytelling / scroll-driven animation** — the page plays a short film as you scroll (`animation-timeline: scroll()`).
- **Parallax** — background moves slower than foreground. Easy to overdo.
- **WebGL / Three.js** — real 3D in the browser (aircraft, carton). Heavy; keep off the quote form.
- **Mesh gradient / aurora** — soft overlapping color blobs behind a hero (not a loud wallpaper).
- **Grain / noise** — faint film texture so flat color feels less digital.
- **Dark cinematic mode** — near-black stage, one spotlight subject (login or a quote-share page).
- **View transitions** — the browser cross-fades between screens (`document.startViewTransition`).
- **Custom cursor / magnetic hover** — pointer sticks to buttons. Skip on desks; it fights data entry.

Apple’s public sites mix **restraint + a few of these**. The “wow” is not a secret toolkit.

## Brand

Market brand is **Vertex**. Do not show Namahsky Nexus in UI.
